gm-method: a tooling defect worked-around (e.g. trimming state to dodge a wasm error) is NOT permanent - fix it at the SOURCE repo that cascades. Find the source: the deployed wrapper in ~/.gm-tools comes from the published npm package, NOT necessarily the obvious rs-* repo; read the published package's package.json `repository` field (here AnEntrypoint/gm, dir gm-plugkit) to find the real source, and confirm via `gh run list --workflow publish.yml` that a push auto-bumps+publishes (the cascade). ROOT CAUSE of the 'Start offset -N is outside the bounds of the buffer' on every wasm verb once state grew large: a DETACHED ArrayBuffer, NOT a signed-pointer decode. When wasm linear memory grows (a big plugkit_alloc for large input), any previously-captured `instance.exports.memory.buffer` DETACHES (byteLength->0), so a `new Uint8Array(staleBuffer, ptr, len)` for a valid post-grow ptr throws. FIX: always re-read instance.exports.memory.buffer FRESH at the moment of each view, never reuse a buffer captured across an alloc/dispatch (memory-grow) boundary; consolidate the repeated (ptr,len) decode into ONE helper so the fix can't drift back. (Note: JS BigInt masking `(neg) & 0xffffffffn` already handles a signed-i64 correctly, so asUintN normalization is harmless hardening, not the root cause - witness with a tiny test before blaming the decode.) WITNESS the wasm-memory bug standalone: `const m=new WebAssembly.Memory({initial:1,maximum:100}); const b=m.buffer; m.grow(50); new Uint8Array(b,1000,16)` throws 'detached ArrayBuffer'; re-reading m.buffer after grow reads fine. Ship it as a `.test.js` that the source repo's CI runs so the regression is permanently guarded. CAVEAT: a `git reset --hard` / process churn in the consuming repo can kill the spool watcher mid-session - boot a fresh one (it loads the now-fixed wrapper) and re-dispatch.